{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "lib": [
      "ES2020",
      "DOM"
    ],
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "strict": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    // TypeScript 7 no longer auto-includes every package under node_modules/@types the way 5.x
    // did, so @types/node has to be named explicitly or parity.test.ts — which reads
    // BlazorViewRegistry.cs off disk — loses `node:fs` / `node:path` (TS2591). Same fix, same
    // reason, as clients/react's tsconfig; it is what the compiler's own diagnostic prescribes,
    // and naming one entry is stricter than 5.x's include-everything.
    "types": [
      "node"
    ],
    // No `baseUrl`: TypeScript 7 REMOVED it (TS5102). The `paths` below are all relative, and
    // without a baseUrl tsc resolves them against this file's directory — which is exactly what
    // `baseUrl: "."` meant here.
    // 🚨 ONE React type identity for the whole program — the tsc equivalent of the bundler
    // dedupe portal-next does with webpack resolve.modules (and Vite's `dedupe`).
    // "@meshweaver/react/core" below source-aliases the SHARED renderer, whose own
    // clients/react/node_modules holds a DIFFERENT @types/react. Without the react/* entries the
    // program carries TWO React type identities and every JSX call site fails with
    //   TS2786: 'RenderArea' cannot be used as a JSX component
    //     ... Type 'bigint' is not assignable to type 'ReactNode'
    // (React 19's ReactNode admits bigint, React 18's does not) — 28 errors, none of them a real
    // incompatibility. A RESOLUTION fix, not a suppression: it silences nothing, and the shared
    // core was verified to typecheck cleanly against EITHER copy (18 or 19), so it is not masking
    // a version requirement. It survives the Expo SDK 57 / RN 0.86 upgrade (#1584) unchanged,
    // exactly as predicted: both packages are on React 19 now and the dedupe is STILL required,
    // because the problem is two node_modules trees sharing source, not a version skew.
    "paths": {
      "@meshweaver/react/core": [
        "../react/src/core.ts"
      ],
      "@meshweaver/client-web": [
        "../grpc-web/src/index.ts"
      ],
      "react": [
        "./node_modules/@types/react"
      ],
      "react/jsx-runtime": [
        "./node_modules/@types/react/jsx-runtime"
      ],
      "react-dom": [
        "./node_modules/@types/react-dom"
      ]
    }
  },
  "include": [
    "App.tsx",
    "src"
  ],
  "extends": "expo/tsconfig.base"
}
